#edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124617 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272188 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124617 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124617 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186411615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124617 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Steady-State Vector of a Stochastic 3x3 Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124617 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124428 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124617 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "84798595-cdd4-57bf-953f-0856b041b65f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124712 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125565 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124712 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125566 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124712 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125567 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124712 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124712 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125569 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124712 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125570 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124712 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125588 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124712 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124712 #edb/ref 1045 "Steady-State Vectors" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124712 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186125588 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124712 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048393 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124712 #edb/ref 1050 960.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124712 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "13665af8-c926-5261-b3fc-925c516a2fd1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124773 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124773 #edb/ref 1069 "q-110243" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124773 #edb/ref 1071 "$P = \\begin{bmatrix}0.9 & 0.2 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.2 & 0.1 \\\\ 0 & 0.6 & 0.6\\end{bmatrix}, q = \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nGiven that $q$ is the steady-state vector of the stochastic matrix $P$ shown above, what is the value of $a + b - c$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124773 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9d9e86d2-c897-5b07-851a-148dd18a6f1a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124775 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124775 #edb/ref 1069 "q-111192" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124775 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124775 #edb/ref 1071 "A particular town has a fleet of $720$ bicycles for rent. The table below gives the pattern of rental and return locations over a typical day. \n\n|  |  | From: |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Museum** | **Station** | **Gym** |\n| To: | **Museum** | $0.9$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ |\n| **Station** | $0$ | $0.3$ | $0.7$ |  |\n| **Gym** | $0.1$ | $0.4$ | $0.2$ |  |\n\nFor example, $40\\%$ of the bicycles at the Station are moved to the Gym every day.\n\nHow many bicycles would eventually be located at the Station if the listed probabilities remained constant over many days?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186124775 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cac3744a-0cdf-56d6-88ab-9bba2eefa254" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124775 #edb/ref 1074 "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.9 & 0.3 & 0.1 \\\\ 0 & 0.3 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.4 & 0.2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix ${P}^{2}$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. \nAs a result, after many years, the distribution will eventually converge to the steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0.9 & 0.3 & 0.1 \\\\ 0 & 0.3 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.4 & 0.2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.1 & 0.3 & 0.1 \\\\ 0 & -0.7 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.4 & -0.8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.1 & 0.3 & 0.1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -0.7 & 0.7 & 0 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.4 & -0.8 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=10{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=10{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=10{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -8 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -7 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={x}_{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-{x}_{1}+3({x}_{3})+{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=4{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{3}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ 6{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{6} \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, after many days, $\\frac{1}{6}$ of the bicycles will be at the station. Therefore, the total number of bikes at the station after many days will be\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 720=120.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186125565 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125566 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125565 #edb/ref 1061 34230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125565 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "965fe16f-9c57-5058-9e32-90332314977c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125565 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125566 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125567 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125566 #edb/ref 1061 34231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125566 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bd127c03-d025-5b21-ae93-e24b7aec916f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125566 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125567 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125570 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125567 #edb/ref 1061 34232 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125567 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "429ea8df-33bc-5c79-a0d6-97b71166cd98" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125567 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125568 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125569 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125568 #edb/ref 1061 34233 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125568 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8ba24a30-9b4e-5e58-93b1-b999bb45fe16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125568 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125569 #edb/ref 1061 34234 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125569 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3865f17c-09ee-5b73-96f9-45f1dd985dd9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125569 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124617 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125570 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125570 #edb/ref 1061 34235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125570 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "735b7b96-43fa-5c0e-b4b8-df7598a6610b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125570 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125588 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125565 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125588 #edb/ref 1061 37332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125588 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7c429a3d-9dd8-5fe2-a7e7-cf0e9f515d1a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125588 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125849 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we have a stochastic matrix $P$ and a stochastic vector $\\mathbf{q}.$ Then, $\\mathbf{q}$ is a **steady-state vector** of $P$ if \n\n$$\nP\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{q}.\n$$\n\nFor example, to show that the stochastic vector \n![](images/ca/caba3c1b2f3245a3ebea0bd6ff6d47c63d409a7ba18a48db1f1269cb9ac13854.svg) \n\nis a steady-state vector for the stochastic matrix \n![](images/7f/7f231d09e7daf85eec90e682a9eb53c242aadac75b372e3685b65757ae3309dc.svg) \n\nwe compute the matrix product $P\\mathbf q\\mathbin:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =[\\begin{aligned}0.94 & 0.03 \\\\ 0.06 & 0.97\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0.94 & 0.03 \\\\ 0.06 & 0.97\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0.94+2\\cdot 0.03 \\\\ 0.06+2\\cdot 0.97\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =q\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $P\\mathbf q = \\mathbf q,$ the vector $\\mathbf q$ is a steady-state vector of $P.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125849 #edb/ref 1065 5500 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125849 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e1601d67-32bc-5796-a800-6e704f25f991" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125849 #edb/ref 1067 "Steady-State Vectors" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125850 #edb/ref 1064 "To find a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P,$ we seek a stochastic vector $\\mathbf{q}$ such that \n\n$$\nP\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{q}.\n$$\n\nLet's begin by writing this equation in a slightly different form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector we seek is a solution to the matrix equation $(P-I)\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{0}.$\n\nLet's use this idea to find a steady-state vector for the matrix\n![](images/77/77ffe4078d3c62c30ab94b59bc9465a92db659b9e70b137bb144deb44844e800.svg)\n\nFirst, we compute $(P-I):$\n![](images/3d/3d91ece6103baa469569adf1994f3965cfc3429fbe65c0d7cfffff53b83b20ef.svg)\n\nThen, to solve $(P-I)\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{0},$ we reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.75 & 0.5 & 0 \\\\ 0.75 & -0.5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-0.75 & 0.5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\nx_1= \\dfrac 23 x_2.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n![](images/4a/4a5d81320f3e4eeee6b9fb09424238467d6c9e8b2e2246971ba3550b65a6d9c2.svg)\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{3}{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, our steady-state vector is\n\n![](images/85/8515cb27fa64a5186fae0c63184df2ff7651c03963eef5804c4d6bedbace2283.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125850 #edb/ref 1065 5501 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125850 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "78f635ff-e2e7-5c9f-9ec2-470187a45bc3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125850 #edb/ref 1067 "Finding the Steady-State Vector of a Stochastic Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125851 #edb/ref 1064 "A stochastic matrix $P$ is **regular** if there exists a positive integer $n$ such that $P^n$ has all positive elements.\n\nLet's consider some examples:\n\n- The stochastic matrix $P,$ given by\n![](images/e9/e9588bb9c77762d6caee5460403077eba65a891676f85a9e30ff99e7e6112184.svg) \nhas all positive elements. Therefore, it is a regular stochastic matrix.\n\n- The stochastic matrix $Q,$ given by\n![](images/e0/e0ba33618820d19e77d365a9f316239ef2e36133cbcac4007bf2b5c2f6e57137.svg) \ncontains a zero element. However, if we compute its square, we get\n![](images/29/29c5a411ac3c797699a39e35972e415e98df3347e66581bb5494f61a92d92bd5.svg) \nwhich has all positive elements. Therefore, $Q$ is a regular stochastic matrix.\n\nRegular stochastic matrices have the following important property:\n\n*Any regular stochastic matrix has a **unique** steady-state vector $\\mathbf q.$ Moreover, the Markov chain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0}, \\\\ {x}_{2} & =P{x}_{1}, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =P{x}_{2}, \\\\ ⋮ & \\,⋮ \\\\ {x}_{k+1} & =P{x}_{k} \\\\ ⋮ & \\,⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nconverges to $\\mathbf q$ for **any** initial state $\\mathbf x_0.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125851 #edb/ref 1065 5502 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125851 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "255fadf0-9fc2-52d5-976b-015a97f52b08" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125851 #edb/ref 1067 "Regular Stochastic Matrices" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125855 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we have a population where people live either in the city or the countryside. A study carried out by the local authority shows that, each year,\n\n- $6\\%$ of the city population relocates to the countryside,\n\n- $3\\%$ of the countryside population relocates to the city, and\n\n- everyone else remains where they are.\n\nAdditionally, $70\\%$ of the population lived in the city and $30\\%$ in the countryside at the time of the study. \n\nWe can represent this situation using a stochastic matrix $P$ and initial state vector $\\mathbf x_0,$ as follows:\n\n![](images/44/44fa9ad32622bd1d7b3541206030ecc862a3db9420f46049c9f16992fdb947ca.svg) \n\nAs we've seen, to find the state vector $\\mathbf x_{k+1}$ that corresponds to the $(k+1)$th year, we left-multiply the state vector corresponding to the $k$th year by $P.$ This gives rise to the following Markov chain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0}, \\\\ {x}_{2} & =P{x}_{1}, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =P{x}_{2}, \\\\ ⋮ & \\,⋮ \\\\ {x}_{k+1} & =P{x}_{k} \\\\ ⋮ & \\,⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe local authority wants to determine whether the state vectors settle down to a fixed state. With the aid of a computer, they compute the following states:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}0.667 \\\\ 0.333\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {x}_{2} & =P{x}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0.636\\,97 \\\\ 0.363\\,03\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {x}_{3} & =P{x}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0.609\\,64 \\\\ 0.390\\,36\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{100} & =P{x}_{99}=[\\begin{aligned}0.333\\,363 \\\\ 0.666\\,637\\end{aligned}]≈[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {x}_{101} & =P{x}_{100}=[\\begin{aligned}0.333\\,36 \\\\ 0.666\\,64\\end{aligned}]≈[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the data, we see that the vectors $\\mathbf x_{100}$ and $\\mathbf x_{101}$ are both approximately equal to the vector\n\n![](images/03/030d5719a268d9e15c2c58488ff1c36602d5c757475e79c25dcd1da5a4eb8712.svg)\n\nFurthermore, computing $\\mathbf x_{102}, \\mathbf{x}_{103}, \\ldots$ all give vectors approximately equal to $\\mathbf q.$ This suggests that the sequence $\\mathbf x_k$ converges to $\\mathbf q\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx_k \\rightarrow \\mathbf q \\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad k\\to\\infty.\n$$\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf q$ is called a **steady-state vector** of the matrix $P.$ Here, our steady-state vector suggests that after enough time has passed, one-third of the population will live in the city and two-thirds will live in the countryside.\n\nFinally, notice that for large values of $k,$ we have\n\n$$\nP\\mathbf x_k \\approx \\mathbf x_{k}.\n$$\n\nThis observation indicates how to find $\\mathbf q$ without resorting to computers." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125855 #edb/ref 1065 6151 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125855 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "cb9435c4-44ed-58ec-9c04-b8fed4403fcd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125855 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244765 #edb/ref 1080 "60" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244765 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244765 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "327c20aa-5b6d-5d5d-95d5-67fe1edd6dda" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244781 #edb/ref 1080 "30" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244781 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244781 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "33ffc812-4891-586d-bc0d-281bd530059e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246007 #edb/ref 1080 "240" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246007 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246007 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "be00ba0f-33e3-51bb-b788-c863cec54aad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246016 #edb/ref 1080 "120" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246016 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246016 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "beae8b6f-7eef-5f2b-b111-68f42f84b6fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246357 #edb/ref 1080 "480" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246357 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246357 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e6a13eed-4a8b-5532-9d37-57dbd1d42511" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246913 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186244765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246913 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186244781 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246913 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186246007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246913 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186246016 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246913 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186246357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246913 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246913 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246913 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186246016 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246913 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "cb514e9d-3fa7-5bab-af7e-44674ca75bc0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266379 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{2}{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266379 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266379 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1b828146-3af8-5909-903e-b51a9b905309" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266530 #edb/ref 1080 "65\\,000" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266530 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266530 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2ba600a6-1907-5e14-b550-b044551ef5e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266597 #edb/ref 1080 "85\\,000" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266597 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266597 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "33aab18e-ac2a-5fe2-8c2c-6bbd3d17578b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186267042 #edb/ref 1080 "80\\,000" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186267042 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186267042 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "656ef5ce-de1c-585b-b58e-bcb0488a1f4d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186267200 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{1}{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186267200 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186267200 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "783f6a21-5d08-51b0-a129-e4614e791183" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186267205 #edb/ref 1080 "90\\,000" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186267205 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186267205 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "78a20fcd-e709-5231-beab-5b7abd75b2d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186267313 #edb/ref 1080 "75\\,000" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186267313 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186267313 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "84bfcca2-04fa-5a72-b5ec-b6d4efc25021" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186267404 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186267404 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186267404 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8d08161d-17c7-50f2-91f4-495659a8a63b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268349 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{1}{9}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268349 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268349 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f8ca4cde-be3a-5390-b261-7492120df2ce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268382 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{3}{5}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268382 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268382 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fe1eb026-f8a8-5839-b850-5db4012183c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268487 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186266379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268487 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186267200 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268487 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186267404 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268487 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186268349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268487 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186268382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268487 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268487 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268487 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186268349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268487 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "2e83eae8-e4dc-5643-b310-0c6ec7afc244" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268607 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186266530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268607 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186266597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268607 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186267042 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268607 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186267205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268607 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186267313 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268607 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268607 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268607 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186267313 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268607 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "6e7c105c-3db3-51ac-83dc-0a87716a4e48" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269030 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269030 #edb/ref 1069 "q-135323" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269030 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269030 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.25 & 0.6 \\\\ 0.75 & 0.4\\end{bmatrix},\\,q=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven that $q$ is the steady-state vector of the stochastic matrix $P,$ what is the value of $a-b?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186269030 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d5136e32-dc7f-55de-b02b-4d944cd2c122" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269030 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0.25 & 0.6 \\\\ 0.75 & 0.4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.75 & 0.6 \\\\ 0.75 & -0.6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.75 & 0.6 & 0 \\\\ 0.75 & -0.6 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-0.75 & 0.6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-0.75{x}_{1}+0.6{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 75{x}_{1}-60{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{9}{5}{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{5}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{9} \\\\ \\frac{5}{9}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $a-b=\\frac{4}{9}-\\frac{5}{9}=-\\frac{1}{9}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186269057 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268607 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269057 #edb/ref 1069 "q-135409" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269057 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269057 #edb/ref 1071 "A factory produces two types of sugar: brown and extra-refined. Every year, the factory's sales executives study how customers' preferences change between the two types of sugar. The table below shows the results of this study.\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Brown** | **Extra-Refined** |\n| To: | **Brown** | $0.8$ | $1$ |\n| **Extra-Refined** | $0.2$ | $0$ |  |\n\nIt's found that $20\\%$ of customers who currently buy brown sugar will switch to extra-refined sugar next year, while $100\\%$ of customers who currently purchase extra-refined sugar will switch to brown sugar next year.\n\nAssuming that there are $450\\,000$ customers in total and that the given probabilities remain constant over many years, how many customers will eventually consume extra-refined sugar?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186269057 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b9da89ba-6ad8-53a7-ac87-9c6b7aab9eb4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269057 #edb/ref 1074 "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.8 & 1 \\\\ 0.2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n{P}^{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.84 & 0.8 \\\\ 0.16 & 0.2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${P}^{2}$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. As a result, after many years, the distribution will eventually converge to the unique steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0.8 & 1 \\\\ 0.2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.2 & 1 \\\\ 0.2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0.2 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-0.2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=5{x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ 6{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{6} \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{1}{6}$ of the customers will eventually use extra-refined sugar. Therefore, the number of customers who will eventually use this type of sugar is\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 450\\,000=75\\,000.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186408166 #edb/ref 10 :answer-field.type/checkbox #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186411596 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186411618 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411596 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186411619 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411596 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186411620 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411596 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411596 #edb/ref 1045 "Steady-State Vectors" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411596 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186411620 #edb/ref 17592186045417